p("**IaC tool performance.** Regvart et al. (2026) is the closest precedent for this study in architecture and method. Implementing the same AWS architecture in three tools and deploying and destroying it thirty times each under identical conditions (same account, region, credentials, machine and network), the authors found Terraform and Pulumi close in provisioning time and CloudFormation roughly 2.4 times slower, and observed that in pipelines billed per runner-minute a difference in duration is a difference in cost. The study answers which tool to choose, not whether to adopt IaC at all, and it does not place the tools in a pipeline."),
p("**IaC quality, security and maintenance.** A large body of work shows that writing infrastructure as code does not make it good code. Rahman, Mahdavi-Hezaveh, and Williams (2019) mapped the field and found defects and security under-studied; the same group then catalogued seven security smells across 15,232 scripts, including 1,326 hard-coded passwords (Rahman, Parnin, & Williams, 2019), linked script size and hard-coded strings to defects (Rahman & Williams, 2019), and built an eight-category defect taxonomy in which configuration-data errors were most frequent (Rahman et al., 2020). For Terraform specifically, Verdet et al. (2025) found that across 812 projects access controls were adopted but encryption at rest was neglected, and Begoug, Ouni, and Chouchen (2025) and Begoug, Ouni, and Khelifi (2025) showed that module and AWS-provider dependencies lag upstream releases by about ten and nine months respectively. Practitioner sources agree: Guerriero et al. (2019) found tool support for testing infrastructure code lacking, Kumara et al. (2021) catalogued recurring good and bad practices, and Begoug et al. (2023) found that deployment pipelines were among the most difficult IaC topics on Stack Overflow. Kolomytskyi et al. (2026), in a small single-case study in a lower-profile venue, reported that only a gated, policy-checked workflow caught all violations before apply, against half for a GitOps pipeline and none for the plain Terraform command line, a result directly relevant to integration depth."),
p("**CI/CD and GitHub Actions.** Hilton et al. (2016) and Vasilescu et al. (2015) established the benefits of continuous integration for application code, and Shahin et al. (2017) synthesised the continuous-practices literature, identifying secure pipeline design as an open problem. GitHub Actions has since become dominant: Kinsman et al. (2021) studied its early adoption, Decan et al. (2022) found it in 43.9% of 69,147 active repositories, and Rostami Mazrae et al. (2023) explained, from 22 practitioner interviews, why teams migrated to it and what made the migration hard, notably the learning curve of a new YAML syntax and the impossibility of testing a workflow locally, which forces a commit-push-wait cycle for every correction. Decan et al. (2022) also found that only 1.7% of steps pinned third-party actions to a commit, a reproducibility and security concern that parallels the dependency lag found in Terraform."),
p("**DevOps and cloud architecture.** Leite et al. (2019) mapped DevOps concepts across process, people, delivery and runtime, situating IaC as a delivery capability. Villamizar et al. (2017) deployed one application three ways on AWS and showed that architecture decisions carry measurable cost consequences, with provider-managed scaling cutting cost substantially; this supports the case architecture's use of AWS Fargate rather than self-managed instances, and supplies a second precedent for deploying the same system several ways under identical conditions."),
h3("2.5.2 Evidence from Southeast Asia"),
p("Regional evidence on IaC is very limited. Searches combining Terraform or IaC with Southeast Asia, Cambodia, Vietnam, Thailand, Indonesia or the Philippines returned no peer-reviewed empirical study measuring IaC adoption or performance in an organisation in the region. Regional literature on cloud computing addresses adoption intention among individuals and small enterprises using the acceptance frameworks discussed in Section 2.3.4, and therefore examines perceptions rather than provisioning behaviour. Because no regional baseline exists, this study compares its results with the international studies above, accepting that differences in team size, tooling maturity and regulation may account for some divergence."),
h3("2.5.3 Evidence from Cambodia"),
p("To the researcher's knowledge, no published study examines IaC, Terraform or CI/CD practice in a Cambodian organisation. The Cambodian technology literature that does exist concerns digital financial services, mobile payments and public-sector information systems, and addresses users rather than engineering practice. Supervisory expectations from the National Bank of Cambodia for controlled change management in financial institutions make version-control coverage a compliance measure rather than a convenience in this setting, a framing that does not appear in the international literature, which draws mainly on open-source projects with no such obligation."),
